Copyright (c) 2026 Jonathan Martín (Fervon). All rights reserved.

Veredicto is source-available commercial software. The source is published so you
can read exactly what runs inside your CI — that is the point of a tool that
inspects your diffs — but reading it is not the same as being licensed to run it.


VERSIONS UP TO AND INCLUDING v0.3.3 REMAIN MIT

Every release up to and including v0.3.3, and every commit up to and including
52f3544, was published under the MIT licence. That grant is irrevocable and is
not withdrawn here: anyone who obtained that code keeps their MIT rights to it,
forever. This licence governs v0.4.0 onwards only.


WHAT YOU MAY DO WITHOUT A LICENCE KEY

  - Read, clone and fork this repository.
  - Run the test suite and evaluate the code locally.
  - Quote it, review it, write about it, teach from it.
  - Submit contributions (see CONTRIBUTING.md).

WHAT REQUIRES A VALID LICENCE KEY

  - Running Veredicto against a repository, in CI or anywhere else, whether or
    not that repository is public and whether or not the run is automated.

A key is issued per repository, or per owner. Keys are sold self-serve at
https://fervon.dev/veredicto/ — there is no sales process to sit through.

WHAT YOU MAY NOT DO

  - Remove, disable, or circumvent the licence check in src/entitlement.js, or
    distribute a modified copy with it removed or weakened.
  - Redistribute, sublicense, resell or host this software as a service.
  - Use it to build a competing product.

The licence check is deliberately simple and entirely offline: it verifies a
signed key locally and never contacts a server, because nothing about your code
should leave your runner. It is not copy protection. Anyone determined to strip
it out can. The enforcement here is legal, and the check exists so that honest
users are never in any doubt about where they stand.


